---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import { pages } from '../i18n/en'
import { href, REPO_URL } from '../lib/paths'

const P = pages.howPage
const article = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'TechArticle',
  headline: 'How SahiSize hits an exact KB size in the browser',
  inLanguage: 'en-IN',
}
---

<Base title={P.title} description={P.description} jsonLd={[article]} breadcrumbs={[{ name: 'Home', href: href() }, { name: 'How it works', href: href('how-it-works') }]}>
  <article class="prose-site max-w-3xl">
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-extrabold">{P.h1}</h1>
    <p class="text-lg muted">
      A portal rule like “photo 200 × 230 px, 20–50 KB, JPG” sounds simple. Meeting it reliably, on a cheap phone, without sending the photo anywhere, takes a
      few tricks. Here they are.
    </p>

    <h2>1. What “KB” means</h2>
    <p>
      Some portals count 1 KB as 1000 bytes, most as 1024. A file of 50,500 bytes is 49.3 KB to one and 50.5 KB to the other. SahiSize aims inside both
      readings: at least <em>min × 1024</em> bytes and at most <em>max × 1000</em> bytes, with a small margin so a strict “less than” check passes too.
    </p>

    <h2>2. Finding the best quality that fits</h2>
    <p>
      JPEG size grows with the encoder’s quality setting (1–100). SahiSize binary-searches it: try 100, try the lowest, then halve the gap until it finds the highest
      quality still under the limit. That takes about eight encodes, and every result is remembered. If even the lowest quality is too big and the portal accepts a
      range of sizes, it tries a slightly smaller picture instead, because a smaller sharp image beats a big blocky one.
    </p>

    <h2>3. When the file is too small</h2>
    <p>
      “Signature 10–20 KB” is the classic trap: a clean 140 × 60 signature is only a few KB even at the best quality. A JPEG is a list of segments, and one kind,
      the comment segment (marker <code>FF FE</code>), is skipped by every decoder. SahiSize adds comment segments filled with spaces until the file reaches the
      minimum. The picture is byte-for-byte the same; the tests decode both files and compare every pixel. PDFs get the same treatment with an XMP metadata packet,
      which reserves whitespace padding by design.
    </p>

    <h2>4. Reading the file, not its name</h2>
    <p>
      A PNG renamed to <code>.jpg</code> still starts with the PNG signature, and portals that check those first bytes reject it. SahiSize reads the first bytes
      (“magic numbers”) of every file. For JPEGs it walks the header segments to read the size, the DPI (from the JFIF header), the colour model (CMYK files
      fail on many portals) and the EXIF rotation tag. Phones often store a photo sideways with a “rotate me” tag that some portals ignore. SahiSize applies the
      rotation to the pixels and writes a file without the tag.
    </p>

    <h2>5. Resizing the same way in every browser</h2>
    <p>
      Browsers resize images differently, and big reductions (a 12-megapixel photo down to 200 × 230) can look jagged. SahiSize does it itself with area
      averaging: every output pixel is the exact average of the source area it covers. It streams rows through a small cache, so memory stays low even for huge
      photos, which are also decoded straight to a manageable size.
    </p>

    <h2>6. Cleaning up a signature photo</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Estimate the paper’s brightness everywhere: the brightest pixel in each small block is paper, because ink strokes are thin. Smooth that map.</li>
      <li>Divide every pixel by it. Shadows and uneven light disappear: paper becomes white everywhere, only ink stays dark.</li>
      <li>Split paper from ink with Otsu’s method, softly, so stroke edges stay smooth.</li>
      <li>Drop specks, find the ink’s bounding box, and crop to it so the signature fills its box (SSC wants at least 80%).</li>
      <li>Repaint the ink in one even colour and fit it into the required size without stretching.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>7. PDFs under a limit</h2>
    <p>
      If a PDF already fits, it is left alone. Otherwise SahiSize first tries a lossless re-save, which keeps the text. If that’s not enough, it re-draws each page
      as a JPEG with a shared budget: each page gets what’s left divided by the pages left, so a blank page leaves room for a busy one. If any page can’t fit,
      the whole document steps down a resolution (150, 120, 100, 85, 72 DPI) and tries again. Password-protected PDFs (like e-Aadhaar) are opened with your
      password and saved without one, because portals reject locked files.
    </p>

    <h2>8. Keeping it private</h2>
    <p>
      All of this runs in Web Workers inside your browser. The page’s security policy blocks every network request, the workers delete their own network
      functions before starting, and there is no server to send anything to. See <a href={href('privacy')}>Privacy</a>.
    </p>
    <p>The code is open source: <a href={REPO_URL} rel="noopener">{REPO_URL.replace('https://', '')}</a>.</p>
  </article>
</Base>
